			"# Color\n\nUse color tokens to communicate intent, hierarchy, and interaction in Atlassian UI. Start with the\nexisting ADS component treatment; use these choices when styling custom UI or reviewing a token\nchoice.\n\n## Choose the token family\n\n1. Identify what the color communicates. Use a semantic role for status, validation, actions, or\n   discovery. Use an accent when the hue identifies a category and could change without changing its\n   meaning. See the Color accents guidance for hue and pairing choices.\n2. Choose the background emphasis that fits the intended hierarchy.\n3. Pair it with the text, icon, and border tokens for that role and emphasis.\n4. For interactive UI, apply the state to the element that owns the interaction. Preserve separate\n   cues for persistent selection and keyboard focus.\n\nApply tokens with `token('color...')` from `@atlaskit/tokens`. Tokens resolve for light and dark\nthemes, so use their semantic names instead of copying palette values or hardcoded colors.\n\n## Read token names\n\nMost tokens follow `color.[property].[role].[emphasis].[state]`:\n\n- **Property** describes where the color is applied: `background`, `text`, `icon`, or `border`.\n- **Role** describes its intent: `brand`, `danger`, `warning`, `success`, `information`,\n  `discovery`, `neutral`, `selected`, `focused`, `input`, or `accent.[hue]`.\n- **Emphasis** describes contrast against the default surface: `bold`, `bolder`, `boldest`,\n  `subtle`, `subtler`, or `subtlest`.\n- **State** describes an interaction state such as `hovered` or `pressed`.\n\nAccent tokens place the hue after the role, for example `color.background.accent.blue.subtlest`.\nLook up exceptions such as `color.link`, `color.blanket`, `color.skeleton`, and `color.chart.*` in\nthe token catalog rather than extending the pattern.\n\n## Choose a semantic role\n\n| Role           | Choose it for                                     | Example use                         |\n| :------------- | :------------------------------------------------ | :---------------------------------- |\n| `danger`       | Destructive actions, errors, or critical failures | Error message or destructive action |\n| `warning`      | Caution, risk, or something that needs attention  | Risk notice                         |\n| `success`      | A successful, completed, or valid state           | Completion message                  |\n| `information`  | A passive informational message                   | Informational notice                |\n| `brand`        | Primary actions or product identity               | Primary action                      |\n| `discovery`    | Discovery, AI, or a new feature                   | New feature callout                 |\n| `neutral`      | Base surfaces and neutral layout                  | Page or control surface             |\n| `input`        | Form field backgrounds and boundaries             | Text field                          |\n| `selected`     | A persistent current choice                       | Selected row or option              |\n| `focused`      | Keyboard focus indicator; border only             | Focus ring                          |\n| `accent.[hue]` | Categories and labels whose hue is optional       | User-chosen tag                     |\n\nKeep fixed meaning in the semantic role. For example, an error uses `danger` and a successful state\nuses `success`; an accent red or green is for a category whose color does not carry that meaning.\n\n## Pair semantic backgrounds and foregrounds\n\n| Background                                              | Text and icon                                                 |\n| :------------------------------------------------------ | :------------------------------------------------------------ |\n| Semantic `*.bold` or `*.bolder`                         | `color.text.inverse` and `color.icon.inverse`                 |\n| `color.background.warning.bold`                         | `color.text.warning.inverse` and `color.icon.warning.inverse` |\n| Subtle, subtler, subtlest, or default semantic surfaces | Matching role text and icon, such as `color.text.danger`      |\n\nUse the warning-specific inverse pair shown above on a bold warning background. Accent foreground\npairings are in the Color accents guidance.\n\n```tsx\n// Error banner\nbackgroundColor: token('color.background.danger.bold'),\ncolor: token('color.text.inverse'),\n\n// Category label whose hue carries no status meaning\nbackgroundColor: token('color.background.accent.red.bolder'),\ncolor: token('color.text.inverse'),\n```\n\n## Apply state to the interactive element\n\n### Form fields\n\nUse `color.background.input` for the field background, with `color.background.input.hovered` and\n`color.background.input.pressed` for those states. Use `color.border.input` for the field boundary.\nStart with the ADS form component’s treatment when one owns the control.\n\n### Selection and focus\n\nUse `color.background.selected`, `color.text.selected`, `color.icon.selected`, or\n`color.border.selected` for a persistent choice. Use `color.border.focused` for keyboard focus. When\nan element is both selected and focused, keep the selected treatment and add the focus indicator.\nDon’t use link, brand, or focused tokens to communicate selection. Pair selection color with another\ncue, such as a checkmark, underline, border, or shape change, so selection remains clear without\ncolor alone.\n\nApply `hovered` and `pressed` variants to the currently interactive element. Keep a selected item\nrecognizable during those temporary states. Icons do not have dedicated hovered or pressed color\ntokens; use a subtle neutral background such as `color.background.neutral.subtle.hovered` or\n`color.background.neutral.subtle.pressed` when the icon’s control needs a surface response.\n\n## Check the rendered result\n\nInspect the foreground, meaningful borders, and state changes against the final visible background\nin the themes the experience supports. For translucent colors, include the underlying surface when\nchecking contrast. Use `color.chart.*` for chart marks and product UI tokens for axes, labels, and\ngridlines.\n\n## Look up exact tokens\n\nSearch the ADS token registry when an identifier or pairing is uncertain:\n\n```bash\natlas ads search \"danger background\" --type token\natlas ads token color.background.danger.bold --json\n```\n\nIf the Atlas plugin is unavailable, use\n`npx @atlaskit/ads-cli search \"danger background\" --type token`.",
