			"# Color accents\n\nUse accents for categories, projects, and labels when their hue can change without changing their\nmeaning. Choose a semantic color role for status, alerts, validation, primary actions, and discovery\ninstead; see the Color guidance.\n\nStart with the existing ADS component treatment for tags and labels. For custom UI, keep the\nbackground, text, and icon in the same accent hue family.\n\n**Available hues:** `blue`, `teal`, `green`, `lime`, `yellow`, `orange`, `red`, `magenta`, `purple`,\nand `gray`.\n\nPrefer another accent hue, such as orange, to yellow. Yellow accents can appear brown as their\nvalues adjust to meet contrast requirements.\n\n## Pair hue and emphasis\n\n| Background emphasis     | Text and icon                                 |\n| :---------------------- | :-------------------------------------------- |\n| `subtlest` or `subtler` | Matching accent `default` or `bolder`         |\n| `subtle`                | Matching accent `bolder`                      |\n| `bolder`                | `color.text.inverse` and `color.icon.inverse` |\n\n```tsx\n// A category tag: the blue hue identifies a category, not a status\nbackgroundColor: token('color.background.accent.blue.subtlest'),\ncolor: token('color.text.accent.blue'),\n```\n\nUse `color.chart.*` for chart marks. For other pairings, check the rendered foreground and\nbackground: text needs 4.5:1 contrast and meaningful UI boundaries need 3:1.\n\n## Choose borders by their purpose\n\nUse `color.border.accent.[hue]` for a meaningful accent border on a non-bold accent background when\nthe boundary needs to meet 3:1 contrast. Verify the rendered pair when the boundary communicates a\ncontrol or state. The `.subtle` accent border variant is decorative; use it for visual decoration\nthat does not need to identify a boundary or state, not for a required control boundary. A required\nboundary must remain distinguishable without relying on a low-contrast decorative border.\n\n## Verify themes and states\n\nCheck the tag or label in its actual component, theme, and interaction states. Confirm that its hue\nstill means only the category, its text remains legible, and any required boundary stays visible.",
